const intro={"n":9,"title":"Fierro Fights the Police and Meets Sergeant Cruz","summary":"Living as an outlaw, Fierro sleeps outdoors and watches for police patrols. One night, he hears a mounted party approaching. They accuse him of killing two men and attempt to arrest him. Fierro refuses to surrender, and a violent fight follows. At its height, Sergeant Cruz, part of the pursuing force, refuses to let Fierro be killed and joins him against the others. After the battle, Fierro prays for those who died, and the two men seek shelter and begin a friendship. This is the canto in which Cruz joins Fierro, but it does not make the deaths disappear.","people":"Martín Fierro; the members of the pursuing police party; Sergeant Cruz, who intervenes and fights alongside Fierro.","themes":"Outlaw life and isolation; conflict with the police; courage and risk; friendship and solidarity; the cost of violence; responsibility and prayer.","words":"matrero (fugitive or outlaw gaucho); partida (police or armed party); pingo (horse); facón (large rural knife); carabina (carbine); chajá (a South American bird whose cry warns Fierro of movement).","questions":["How do the images of isolation prepare readers for the encounter with the police?","What do the members of the party accuse Fierro of doing?","Why does Cruz change sides, and how does that shape their relationship?","How does Fierro's prayer for the dead complicate the heroic telling of the fight?","How does the friendship between Fierro and Cruz compare with Fierro's later advice about loyalty?"],"nums":[250,264,280],"analysis":"Fierro's descriptions of isolation help explain why he views the police as a threat, but the officers' accusation also recalls the two earlier killings. Cruz's intervention is a dramatic act of solidarity; the source does not require us to regard either man as morally flawless. By ending with an appeal for forgiveness for the dead, the canto brings together action, regret and the wish to survive. Link it to the later lessons on friendship and avoiding violence.","related":"/en/library/old-vizcacha-vs-martin-fierro/","relatedTitle":"A later comparison of solidarity and moral advice"};
---
